On 19–20 June, China’s Taklimakan Desert, one of the world’s largest, experienced a record-breaking downpour that triggered widespread flooding. Steep slopes sent debris-laden run-off downhill, while wind-eroded, salt-crusted surfaces repelled water faster than it could infiltrate, sending floodwaters across the desert’s basin.

